1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,060
I asked Claude, how far is my Goa trip in

2
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:09,380
miles? It searched my own notes,

3
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:11,640
found 650 kilometers,

4
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:14,740
and called a converter to get about 404

5
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:17,960
miles. Both of those tools are tiny Python

6
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:20,740
functions I wrote. The thing that connects

7
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:22,780
them to the AI is called MCP.

8
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:24,340
In the next few minutes,

9
00:00:24,340 --> 00:00:26,280
we build this server from scratch,

10
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:28,280
test it, connect it,

11
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:30,960
and make sure it cannot leak your secrets.

12
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:33,800
MCP, the Model Context Protocol,

13
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:36,640
is an open standard for connecting AI apps

14
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:39,940
to tools and data. Think of it like a USB-C

15
00:00:39,940 --> 00:00:42,940
port for AI. You write a server once,

16
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:44,780
and Claude, VS Code,

17
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:47,680
Cursor, and other apps can all use it.

18
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:49,400
Your server offers tools,

19
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:51,920
and the AI decides when to call them.

20
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:54,100
We will use FastMCP,

21
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:56,760
the most popular Python framework for MCP.

22
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:59,000
It is on version 4 now.

23
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:00,700
Following my uv video,

24
00:01:00,980 --> 00:01:02,800
this is a single file script,

25
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:08,340
uv add --script server.py fastmcp.

26
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:10,980
That writes the dependency into the file,

27
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:13,560
so there is no virtual environment to manage.

28
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:15,300
Here is the whole server.

29
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:19,120
We create a FastMCP app called My Notes.

30
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:21,960
Then each tool is just a Python function

31
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:25,340
with the @mcp.tool decorator.

32
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:28,000
This one searches my notes folder for a

33
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,460
word and returns the matching lines.

34
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:32,060
The important part,

35
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:34,620
the type hints and the docstring are not

36
00:01:34,620 --> 00:01:38,340
decoration here. FastMCP turns them into

37
00:01:38,340 --> 00:01:40,820
the description the AI reads.

38
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:43,680
So a clear docstring is how the AI knows

39
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:45,840
when to use your tool.

40
00:01:46,120 --> 00:01:49,020
The second tool converts kilometers to miles,

41
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:50,460
kilograms to pounds,

42
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:52,160
and Celsius to Fahrenheit.

43
00:01:52,260 --> 00:01:55,060
And at the bottom, mcp.run() starts the

44
00:01:55,060 --> 00:01:56,720
server. That is it.

45
00:01:56,820 --> 00:01:59,120
About 35 lines, two tools.

46
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:00,800
Before connecting an AI,

47
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:04,180
test it yourself. fastmcp list shows both

48
00:02:04,180 --> 00:02:07,060
tools with the inputs the AI will see.

49
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:10,080
fastmcp call runs one directly.

50
00:02:10,180 --> 00:02:13,240
Search for Goa, and it finds my trip plan.

51
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:15,800
Convert 650 kilometers,

52
00:02:16,280 --> 00:02:19,080
and you get 403.9 miles.

53
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:22,520
If this works, the AI side will work too.

54
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:23,960
Now let's connect it.

55
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:26,740
fastmcp install has ready commands for Claude

56
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:29,180
Desktop, Claude Code,

57
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:32,840
Cursor, and more. or mcp-json prints the

58
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:34,960
config to paste into any app.

59
00:02:34,980 --> 00:02:37,200
I gave this config to Claude Code and asked

60
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:39,580
my question. Watch the tool calls.

61
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:41,060
It searched for Goa,

62
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:43,240
then searched again for kilometers,

63
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:44,760
then called convert units.

64
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:47,520
Three calls, all decided by the AI,

65
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:49,940
all running my Python on my machine.

66
00:02:50,060 --> 00:02:52,500
Now, the part most tutorials skip.

67
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:55,080
Here is a read_note tool that takes a file

68
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:57,100
name. Looks harmless,

69
00:02:57,220 --> 00:03:00,420
but ask it for ../.env,

70
00:03:00,860 --> 00:03:02,760
and it walks out of the notes folder and

71
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:04,320
returns my API key.

72
00:03:04,580 --> 00:03:06,880
An AI can be tricked into asking for exactly

73
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:09,740
that. For example, by a hidden instruction

74
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:11,480
in a web page it reads.

75
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:14,440
The fix is two lines: resolve the path,

76
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:16,820
and refuse anything outside the notes folder

77
00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:19,600
or anything that is not a markdown file.

78
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:21,880
Now the same request gets an error.

79
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:24,300
My rules: never expose a tool that runs

80
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:26,600
shell commands or takes any path.

81
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:29,380
Keep tools read only unless they must write,

82
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:31,540
and never put secrets where a tool can reach

83
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:34,000
them. Now a real use case,

84
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:36,620
every small business has a database and

85
00:03:36,620 --> 00:03:39,200
most owners cannot write SQL.

86
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:42,120
So let's give an AI safe access to one.

87
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:44,040
This is a demo shop database,

88
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:49,100
8 products, 12 customers and 360 orders.

89
00:03:49,340 --> 00:03:51,380
The server is just two tools.

90
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:53,560
list_tables returns every table with its

91
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:56,400
columns so the AI can learn the schema by

92
00:03:56,400 --> 00:03:59,820
itself. run_query takes one SQL statement

93
00:03:59,820 --> 00:04:01,980
and returns up to 50 rows.

94
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:03,840
Now notice the two locks.

95
00:04:04,140 --> 00:04:07,240
First, the tool only accepts SELECT queries.

96
00:04:07,460 --> 00:04:09,900
Second, the database file itself is opened

97
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:12,780
read-only. So even if a query slips past

98
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:15,540
the first check, SQLite refuses to write.

99
00:04:15,740 --> 00:04:17,520
Here is how a question flows.

100
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:19,040
You ask in plain English.

101
00:04:19,340 --> 00:04:22,640
The AI calls list_tables to learn the schema,

102
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:24,240
writes the SQL itself,

103
00:04:24,340 --> 00:04:27,080
runs it through run_query and turns the

104
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:28,220
rows into an answer.

105
00:04:28,220 --> 00:04:31,060
I asked Claude, which product made the most

106
00:04:31,060 --> 00:04:34,180
money in October and who was our best customer

107
00:04:34,180 --> 00:04:36,740
that month? It read the schema first,

108
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:39,440
then wrote five queries on its own with

109
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:41,840
joins across all three tables.

110
00:04:42,040 --> 00:04:43,160
Here is one of them.

111
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:45,740
It joins orders, products and customers,

112
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:47,200
filters to October,

113
00:04:47,240 --> 00:04:50,420
skips cancelled orders and sorts by money

114
00:04:50,420 --> 00:04:52,840
spent. I did not write a single line of

115
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:54,760
that SQL. The answer,

116
00:04:55,100 --> 00:04:58,380
the Cold Brew Kit, 19,000 rupees from 20

117
00:04:58,380 --> 00:05:01,020
units. And the best customer was Arjun Patel

118
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:05,540
from Pune with 18,899 rupees.

119
00:05:05,620 --> 00:05:08,640
It even noticed that Masala Chai sold the

120
00:05:08,640 --> 00:05:11,400
most units but came fifth on revenue.

121
00:05:11,780 --> 00:05:13,700
Then I asked it to delete all cancelled

122
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:16,560
orders. Claude stopped and asked me first

123
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:18,880
and pointed out that this connection only

124
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:22,480
allows SELECT. So let's test the locks ourselves.

125
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:25,380
A plain DELETE is refused by the first check.

126
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:27,740
A sneaky one that starts with WITH gets

127
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:29,520
past the first check,

128
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:31,220
but SQLite blocks it.

129
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:33,660
Attempt to write a read-only database.

130
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:36,940
All 360 orders are still there.

131
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:40,500
That is the pattern for any real MCP server.

132
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:42,160
Small, clear tools,

133
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:44,860
a schema the AI can discover and safety

134
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:47,440
enforced in your code and in the system

135
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:49,880
underneath. Not just in the prompt.

136
00:05:50,280 --> 00:05:53,260
Swap SQLite for your company database and

137
00:05:53,260 --> 00:05:55,100
you have an analyst that answers questions

138
00:05:55,100 --> 00:05:58,140
in seconds. The same pattern works for your

139
00:05:58,140 --> 00:06:00,940
expenses sheet, your support tickets or

140
00:06:00,940 --> 00:06:04,240
your server logs. Expose them read-only,

141
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:06,820
describe the tools clearly and let the AI

142
00:06:06,820 --> 00:06:09,530
do the digging. Quick recap,

143
00:06:09,730 --> 00:06:13,330
uv add --script for the setup.

144
00:06:13,330 --> 00:06:16,670
@mcp.tool on a typed function with a good

145
00:06:16,670 --> 00:06:20,030
docstring. fastmcp list and fastmcp call

146
00:06:20,030 --> 00:06:24,350
to test. fastmcp install to connect and

147
00:06:24,350 --> 00:06:26,070
lock down every path.

148
00:06:26,270 --> 00:06:28,310
What tool would you give your AI first?

149
00:06:28,670 --> 00:06:30,830
Tell me in the comments and subscribe for

150
00:06:30,830 --> 00:06:31,810
more modern Python.
